@* The row at the top of a sheet: the grabber, and the button that shuts it.

   Three sheets had this pasted into them -- the rail, PageSheet, and the collection's set picker,
   which cannot use PageSheet because its body is styled on .set-sheet. Identical markup in three
   files is how the four target sheets drifted before PageSheet existed, and changing "Done" to a
   close glyph meant editing the same four lines three times, which is the argument for this being
   a file.

   An X rather than a word. The button dismisses rather than confirming: nothing here is applied on
   the way out -- a filter, a set, a target all take effect the moment they are touched -- so "Done"
   named a step that does not exist and implied one that has to be taken. The glyph also stops the
   button being a label whose width changes with translation, in a row that now has a
   heading in it to make room for.

   Styled by .rail-handle in the stylesheet, which is also where the sheet-only display rule lives:
   a rail that is a column is never an open popover, so it never shows this row at all. *@

<div class="rail-handle">
    <span class="ttl">@Label</span>

    <button class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary sheet-close" popovertarget="@For"
            popovertargetaction="hide" aria-label="@CloseLabel" title="@CloseLabel">&times;</button>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The popover this shuts — the sheet's own id.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string For { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// What this sheet is: "Filters", "Tuning", "Target". The same word its opener carries, so a
    /// sheet says on arrival what you pressed to get it — on a phone every sheet in the app is
    /// otherwise the same panel with the same corners and the same controls.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string Label { get; set; } = "";

    private string CloseLabel => Label is { Length: > 0 } label ? $"Close {label}" : "Close";
}
